Name the core glyph cursors, the way libXcursor names themed ones.

0002 gives the host the name of the cursor under the pointer, which is how the Display page knows
a tap landed on a text field. It only works for clients that load their cursors from an Xcursor
theme: libXcursor is what calls XFixesSetCursorName, and `CursorRec.name` has no other writer. A
prefix with no `share/icons/*/cursors` — a plain Termux install — leaves GTK, Qt and Firefox on
the core font cursors of `XCreateFontCursor`, which the core protocol gives nowhere to put a name,
so every tap reaches the host as "(no name)" and the keyboard never follows the caret.

The name is not really missing, though: it is the glyph index. Glyph N of the "cursor" font is the
shape `XC_<name> == N` of `X11/cursorfont.h`, and libXcursor's theme names are that same list. So
the server names them itself — `left_ptr`, `xterm`, `hand2`, byte for byte what a themed client
would have set — and the existing 0002 path carries them to the host unchanged. No Java change is
needed: the names arrive through `LorieView.onCursorNameChanged`, which already exists.

Where the hook lives. The obvious place is dix's `AllocGlyphCursor`, and a hunk there does apply —
`git apply` at the checkout root writes into a submodule's working tree happily enough (tested on
`xserver/dix/cursor.c`). It is still the wrong place: `xserver` is one of sixteen pinned
freedesktop submodules, and termux-x11's own CMake patches it at configure time
(`lorie/src/main/cpp/patches/xserver.patch`, applied with `patch -N`), so a hunk of ours would be
a second, uncontrolled edit of a tree that is re-fetched on every submodule bump and that
`x11-server/UPSTREAM.md` does not track. Instead lorie's own `InitOutput.c` wraps
`ProcVector[X_CreateGlyphCursor]` from `InitOutput`, and names the cursor after upstream's handler
has created it, found with `dixLookupResourceByType(..., stuff->cid, RT_CURSOR, ...)`. Byte-swapped
clients come through the same entry (`SProcCreateGlyphCursor` tail-calls `ProcVector`), and
ProcVector is not rebuilt per server generation, hence the idempotence check.

What it will and will not name. Only an even glyph below `XC_num_glyphs` of a font whose `FONT`
property is the literal "cursor" — the one core font with no XLFD name, and the cheapest honest
test that the glyphs really are the standard shapes; a client drawing cursors from a font of its
own keeps its own meaning. Odd glyphs are those shapes' masks. A cursor that already carries a
valid name is left alone, so `XFixesSetCursorName` always wins.

Applies after 0001 and 0002; it edits the same file 0002 does, below the block 0002 adds.

See x11-server/UPSTREAM.md.

